{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Cynthia Ann Stephanie "Cyndi" Lauper (born June 22, 1953) is an American singer, songwriter, actress and LGBT rights activist.","title":"Cyndi Lauper"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Her career has spanned over 30 years.","title":"Cyndi Lauper"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Her debut solo album "She's So Unusual" (1983) was the first debut female album to chart four top-five hits on the "Billboard" Hot 100—"Girls Just Want to Have Fun", "Time After Time", "She Bop", and "All Through the Night"—and earned Lauper the Best New Artist award at the 27th Grammy Awards in 1985.","title":"Cyndi Lauper"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Her success continued with the soundtrack for the motion picture "The Goonies" and her second record "True Colors" (1986).","title":"Cyndi Lauper"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" This album included the number one single "True Colors" and "Change of Heart", which peaked at number 3.","title":"Cyndi Lauper"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The True Colors World Tour was a concert tour by American recording artist Cyndi Lauper.","title":"True Colors World Tour"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was Lauper's first headlining world tour in 1986-87 in support of her album, "True Colors".","title":"True Colors World Tour"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The True Colors tour included dates across North America, Asia and Europe.","title":"True Colors World Tour"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Ras Kimono is a Nigerian reggae artist whose debut album Under Pressure led by the single "Rum-Bar Stylée" was a big hit in the Nigerian music scene in 1989.","title":"Ras Kimono"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Before he released his solo album, he was in a group called "The Jastix" along with Amos McRoy and Majek Fashek.","title":"Ras Kimono"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"American singer Beyoncé has embarked on six concert tours during her solo career, four of which have been worldwide and two of which have been collaborative.","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Her solo tour debut (whilst on hiatus with Destiny's Child) began in 2003, with the Dangerously in Love Tour.","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Based predominantly in the United Kingdom, the tour received an unfavorable review from Dave Simpson of "The Guardian" based on Beyoncé's costumes and a confusion of her core audience at the time.","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In 2007, she embarked on her first major solo world tour – The Beyoncé Experience, following Destiny's Child's disbandment in 2005.","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The tour visited five continents and Beyoncé was lauded by music journalism for her simultaneous dancing and singing abilities.","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Following the release of her 2008 third studio album "I Am... Sasha Fierce", Beyoncé embarked on her next world concert venture, the I Am... World Tour.","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" She collaborated with Thierry Mugler exclusively for the tour's costumes.","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The I Am... World Tour also marked the first time Beyoncé had performed in South American countries.","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Beyoncé saw her most commercially successful tour to date with 2013-2014's The Mrs. Carter Show World Tour.","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" Grossing US $229,727,960, the 132 date venture was criticised for the name of the tour, as Beyoncé appeared to be letting herself be known as simply Shawn "Jay-Z" Carter's wife, despite being proclaimed as a "modern-day feminist".","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" The tour itself however was lauded by music critics, who again praised Beyoncé's performance abilities and the more advanced production seen compared to her previous tours.","title":"List of Beyoncé live performances"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"American singer Demi Lovato has embarked six concert tours and performed live at various award ceremonies and television shows.","title":"List of Demi Lovato live performances"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Her debut promotional tour in 2008, Demi Live!","title":"List of Demi Lovato live performances"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Warm Up Tour was based in North America only and supported her debut studio album, "Don't Forget" (2008).","title":"List of Demi Lovato live performances"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" At the same year, Lovato served as one of the opening acts for Jonas Brothers on their fifth concert tour, Burnin' Up Tour.","title":"List of Demi Lovato live performances"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Lovato also served as one of the opening acts for Avril Lavigne on her third concert tour, The Best Damn World Tour on selected dates in North America.","title":"List of Demi Lovato live performances"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" In 2009, Lovato performed as the opening act on the Jonas Brothers World Tour 2009 with Jonas Brothers during the South American and European legs, before she continued to tour her first headlining tour, during Summer 2009, promoting her debut album "Don't Forget" and her sophomore album "Here We Go Again".","title":"List of Demi Lovato live performances"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The tour featured opening acts, David Archuleta, Jordan Pruitt and KSM.","title":"List of Demi Lovato live performances"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" In 2010, Lovato performed as the opening act on Jonas Brothers' Live in Concert World Tour 2010.","title":"List of Demi Lovato live performances"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" On November 1, 2010, Lovato left the tour after a dispute arose to the public light involving her apparently punching one of the dancers of the tour.","title":"List of Demi Lovato live performances"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" After Lovato left, she was interned in a treatment center to seek out help.","title":"List of Demi Lovato live performances"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"True Colors is the second album by American pop singer Cyndi Lauper, released on September 15, 1986.","title":"True Colors (Cyndi Lauper album)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The album produced several hits as "True Colors", "Change of Heart", and "What's Going On" reached the top twenty of the "Billboard" Hot 100, with the first two becoming top 5 hits.","title":"True Colors (Cyndi Lauper album)"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Cyndi Lauper
