{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Warren County Canal was a branch of the Miami and Erie Canal in southwestern Ohio about 20 mi in length that connected the Warren County seat of Lebanon to the main canal at Middletown in the mid-19th century.","title":"Warren County Canal"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Lebanon was at the crossroads of two major roads, the highway from Cincinnati to Columbus (later U.S. Route 42) and the road from Chillicothe to the College Township (Oxford), but Lebanon businessmen and civic leaders wanted better transportation facilities and successfully lobbied for their own canal, part of the canal fever of the first third of the 19th century.","title":"Warren County Canal"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Warren County Canal was never successful, operating less than a decade before the state abandoned it.","title":"Warren County Canal"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Matton Shipyard is a historic shipyard and canal boat service yard located on Van Schaick Island at Cohoes in Albany County, New York.","title":"Matton Shipyard"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It consists of eight extant buildings, various surviving features, and archaeological remains dating to the period 1916 to 1983 when the site functioned as a shipyard, repair facility, and towboat operation on the New York State Barge Canal and Champlain Canal.","title":"Matton Shipyard"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Extant buildings include the office / stores (c. 1916-1917), watchman's building (c. 1936-1943), sheet metal shop (c. 1937-1938), carpenter shop (c. 1916 and 1936-1943), stores building (c. 1941-1943), pipeshop (c. 1936-1943), pitch building (pre-1936), garage (c. 1916), and electric building.","title":"Matton Shipyard"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Also on the property are a flagpole (c. 1916), dock, steel launching ramps, fence, and camels.","title":"Matton Shipyard"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Numerous ruins also occupy the property.","title":"Matton Shipyard"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The