{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Jongsong Peak is a mountain in the "Janak" section of the Himalayas.","title":"Jongsong Peak"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" At 7462 m it is the 57th highest peak in the world, although it is dominated by 3rd highest, Kangchenjunga, 20 km to the south.","title":"Jongsong Peak"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Jongsong's summit is on tri-junction of India, Nepal and China.","title":"Jongsong Peak"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Burji La (or Burji Pass) is a natural pass in mountains between Skardu and Deosai National Park in Gilgit Baltistan, Pakistan.","title":"Burji La"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Its elevation is 4816 meters.","title":"Burji La"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It is famous especially for its beautiful panoramic view of so many mountain peaks, including that of K2, Nanga Parbat, Masherbrum, Chogolisa, Laila Peak, Golden Peak, Gasherbrum I, Gasherbrum II, Gasherbrum IV and a part of Broad Peak mountain.","title":"Burji La"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lenin Peak (Kyrgyz: Ленин Чокусу , "Lenin Çoqusu", لەنىن چوقۇسۇ; Russian: Пик Ленина , "Pik Lenina"; Tajik: қуллаи Ленин, "qulla‘i Lenin/qullaji Lenin" , renamed қуллаи Абӯалӣ ибни Сино (qulla‘i Abûalî ibni Sino) in July 2006), or Ibn Sina (Avicenna) Peak, rises to 7,134 metres (23,406 ft) in Gorno-Badakhshan (GBAO) on the border of Tajikistan and Kyrgyzstan, and is the second-highest point of both countries.","title":"Lenin Peak"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is considered one of the easiest 7000 m peaks in the world to climb and it has by far the most ascents of any 7000 m or higher peak on Earth, with every year seeing hundreds of climbers make their way to the summit.","title":"Lenin Peak"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Lenin Peak is the highest mountain in the Trans-Alay Range of Central Asia, and in the Pamir Mountains in Tajikistan it is exceeded only by Ismoil Somoni Peak (7,495 m).","title":"Lenin Peak"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It was thought to be the highest point in the Pamirs in Tajikistan until 1933, when Ismoil Somoni Peak (known as "Stalin Peak" at the time) was climbed and found to be more than 300 metres higher.","title":"Lenin Peak"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Two mountains in the Pamirs in China, Kongur Tagh (7,649 m) and Muztagh Ata (7,546 m), are higher than the Tajik summits.","title":"Lenin Peak"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Rush Peak is a mountain in the Karakoram mountain range of Central Asia.","title":"Rush Peak"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is located in the Nagar District of Gilgit-Baltistan, Pakistan.","title":"Rush Peak"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" While not a high peak by local standards, it is noteworthy as a remote trekking destination.","title":"Rush Peak"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It can be reached via Nagar Valley and Hoper Village.","title":"Rush Peak"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The peak can be accessed via Hoper Glacier (Bualtar Glacier) and Barpu Glacier.","title":"Rush Peak"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The peak is relatively easy to climb in the summer months and ascents have been made by amateur climbers without proper mountaineering gear.","title":"Rush Peak"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" In winter, access to the mountain is made difficult by heavy snows.","title":"Rush Peak"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The top of the peak provides stunning views of Miar Peak (6,824 m), Miar Glacier and Phuparash Peak (6,574 m).","title":"Rush Peak"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" On a clear day, one can look east and see some of the world's highest mountains, including K2 and Broad Peak.","title":"Rush Peak"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" At the mountain's base lies one of the world's highest alpine lakes, Rush Lake (4,694m).","title":"Rush Peak"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Karakoram, or Karakorum is a large mountain range spanning the borders of Pakistan, India, and China, with the northwest extremity of the range extending to Afghanistan and Tajikistan.","title":"Karakoram"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is located in the regions of Gilgit–Baltistan (Pakistan), Ladakh (India), and southern Xinjiang (China), and reaches the Wakhan Corridor (Afghanistan).","title":"Karakoram"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" A part of the complex of ranges from the Hindu Kush to the Himalayan Range, it is one of the Greater Ranges of Asia.","title":"Karakoram"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The Karakoram is home to the four most closely located peaks over 8000m in height on earth: K2, the second highest peak in the world at 8611 m , Gasherbrum I, Broad Peak and Gasherbrum II.","title":"Karakoram"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Broad Peak (Urdu: ‎ ) is the 12th highest mountain in the world at 8051 m above sea level.","title":"Broad Peak"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The literal translation of "Broad Peak" to Falchan Kangri (ཨིྰན་ཨངརི་) is not accepted among the Balti people.","title":"Broad Peak"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The English name was introduced in 1892 by the British explorer Martin Conway, in reference to the similarly named Breithorn in the Alps.","title":"Broad Peak"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Broad Peak is higher.
